%S A399139 1,2,3,4,5,6,7,7,8,8,9,9,10,10,11,11,12,12,13,13,13,13,14,14,15,15,15,
%T A399139 15,16,16,17,17,17,17,18,18,19,19,19,19,20,20,21,21,21,21,22,22,23,23,
%U A399139 23,23,24,24,24,24,24,24,25,25,26,26,26,26,26,26,27,27,27
%N A399139 Maximum size of a subset of {1,...,n} in which no prime divides more than 3 elements.
%C A399139 Any maximum sized set starts with 1 and the prime powers p, p^2 and p^3 that are at most n. After these elements are chosen, any further admissible number must be the product of two distinct primes greater than n^(1/3). These products can be viewed as edges of a graph. A prime p has capacity 1 when p <= sqrt(n), and capacity 2 when p > sqrt(n). The remaining choices can therefore be viewed as a maximum b-matching problem.
%C A399139 In other words, a(n) = 1 + pi(n) + pi(floor(sqrt(n))) + pi(floor(n^(1/3))) + nu_b(G_n), where G_n is the graph whose vertices are the primes greater than n^(1/3), with p and q joined when p*q <= n. The capacity of a vertex p is 1 if p <= sqrt(n), and 2 otherwise. Here nu_b(G_n) denotes the largest number of edges that can be chosen without using any vertex more often than its capacity allows.
%H A399139 Robert Israel, <a href="/A399139/b399139.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..1000</a>
%H A399139 Hassan Sadeghi, <a href="/A399139/a399139_12.txt">Maple program</a>
%F A399139 a(n) >= 1 + pi(n) + pi(floor(sqrt(n))) + pi(floor(n^(1/3))), where pi = A000720. - _Andrew Howroyd_, Aug 23 2026
%e A399139 For n=7, a(7)=7 and the set {1,2,3,4,5,6,7} is admissible.
%e A399139 For n=8, a(8)=7; the set {1,2,3,4,5,6,7} is a maximum admissible set, because adding 8 would give four selected multiples of the prime 2: 2,4,6,8.
%p A399139 f:= proc(n) uses Optimization;
%p A399139     local P,i,p;
%p A399139     P:= select(isprime, [2,seq(i,i=3..n,2)]):
%p A399139     Maximize(add(x[i],i=1..n),{seq(add(x[i],i=p..n,p)<=3,p=P)},assume=binary,depthlimit=1000)[1];
%p A399139 end proc:
%p A399139 f(1):= 1:
%p A399139 map(f, [$1..100]); # _Robert Israel_, Sep 25 2026
%Y A399139 Cf. A000720, A036234.
%K A399139 nonn,changed
%O A399139 1,2
